TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42983620
TI  - From Satiety to Reward Reshaping: Central Mechanisms of GLP-1 Receptor Agonists and Therapeutic Implications for Reward-related Disorders.
AU  - Liu Yun
AU  - Yu Zhi
AU  - Ng WingYi
AU  - Xie Jiahui
AU  - Wang Yujue
AU  - Yang Guanhu
AU  - Xu Bin
AU  - Xu Tiancheng
JO  - Current neuropharmacology
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.2174/011570159X526080261020103020
AB  - The classical theory of glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1RAs) emphasizes peripheral metabolic regulation and satiety mediated by hypothalamic-brainstem homeostatic pathways. However, numerous clinical studies have shown that GLP-1RAs significantly suppress patients' food cravings and simultaneously reduce cravings for non-food reinforcers such as alcohol and nicotine, suggesting that the effects of GLP-1RAs extend far beyond the regulation of energy balance. GLP-1 receptors are expressed in the ventral tegmental area, the nucleus accumbens, and the central amygdala; their activation exhibits phase-specific effects: during the reward anticipation phase, they reduce dopamine signaling in the nucleus accumbens, whereas during the intake phase, they enhance this signaling. GLP-1RAs can modulate the mesolimbic dopamine system, likely through a combination of direct central actions and indirect peripheral- to-central signaling pathways, dynamically modulating reward processing rather than simply inhibiting it. Given that the mesolimbic dopamine system serves as a common neural substrate for both food and drug addiction, the observed modulatory effects of GLP-1RAs on various reinforcers suggest preliminary potential for expanded application in the treatment of binge eating disorder and substance use disorders, although clinical evidence remains limited for most non-food indications. Future research should elucidate the central penetration characteristics of different agonists, their cell-type-specific mechanisms of action, and the persistence of their neuromodulatory effects to advance the development of precision therapeutic strategies targeting specific reward circuits.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42983620/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42977546
TI  - Semaglutide, Tirzepatide and Clinically Recorded Alcohol-Related Outcomes in Type 2 Diabetes: A Target Trial Emulation.
AU  - Tang Huilin
AU  - Zhang Bingyu
AU  - Lu Ying
AU  - Lu Yiwen
AU  - Wang Haoyuan
AU  - Davis Christal N
AU  - Kranzler Henry R
AU  - Chen Yong
JO  - Diabetes, obesity & metabolism
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1111/dom.71470
AB  - To compare the risk of incident alcohol use disorder (AUD) among adults with Type 2 diabetes (T2D) initiating semaglutide, tirzepatide or sodium-glucose cotransporter-2 inhibitors (SGLT2i). We conducted a target trial emulation using de-identified electronic health record data from the TriNetX research network (June 2022-January 2025). Adults with T2D newly initiating semaglutide, tirzepatide or an SGLT2i were included. The primary outcome was clinically recorded hospitalization or emergency department (ED) visits for AUD. Cox proportional hazards models with 1:1 propensity score matching (PSM) estimated hazard ratios (HRs) and 95% CIs. Sensitivity and subgroup analyses as well as secondary outcomes assessed the robustness of the findings. During a mean follow-up of 0.81-1.02 years, the incidence rates (IRs) of clinically recorded hospitalization or ED visits for AUD were 2.42 versus 4.53 per 1000 person-years for semaglutide versus SGLT2i, with an HR of 0.54 (95% CI, 0.45-0.64). Similarly, the IRs were 2.34 versus 3.98 per 1000 person-years for tirzepatide versus SGLT2i, with an HR of 0.61 (95% CI, 0.46-0.83). In the direct comparison of semaglutide versus tirzepatide, IRs were 2.43 and 2.36 per 1000 person-years, respectively, with no difference in risk (HR, 0.98; 95% CI, 0.73-1.31). Results were consistent across sensitivity and subgroup analyses and secondary outcomes. In adults with T2D, semaglutide and tirzepatide were associated with lower rates of clinically recorded AUD-related outcomes than SGLT2i, with no difference between the two GLP-1RA medications.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42977546/
